WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
- [Narrator] Back in the '90s, most people

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.313
took Pluto for granted.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.733
Fast-forward to 2006.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:13.410
Suddenly, Pluto was all
Americans were talking about.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.790
Or rather, yelling about.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:21.000
Some people were so angry,
they were giving astronomers

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.880
death threats.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.340
- People were so angry that they said that

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.995
all the astronomers should be
put on the wall and shot at.

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:31.210
- [Narrator] That's Thierry Montmerle.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.650
He's former general secretary for the

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.550
International Astronomical Union.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.840
The same organization of
the world's astronomers

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.790
that changed Pluto's status
from planet to dwarf planet

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.130
in 2006.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.170
And today, over 12 years later,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.330
people still have strong opinions

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.783
whenever you ask: Should
Pluto be a planet again?

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:57.803
- No.

00:00:57.803 --> 00:00:58.650
- Yes.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:00:59.483
- No.

00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:00.316
- No.

00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:01.154
- Yeah.

00:01:01.154 --> 00:01:01.987
- Yes.

00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:02.825
- No.

00:01:02.825 --> 00:01:03.767
- Yes.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:05.360
- I'm not really sure
that I have an opinion

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.260
of whether or not Pluto
should be a planet.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.310
- [Narrator] There's
obviously some confusion

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.140
going on here.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.330
So we did the next logical step.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.660
We went to the experts to settle this

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.223
once and for all.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.340
That's Alan Stern.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.060
He leads NASA's New Horizons mission,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.383
which flew by Pluto in 2015.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.070
- And in planetary
science, where the experts

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:35.180
in planets are, we call
small planets "planets."

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.220
We call large moons "planets."

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:41.480
We call all the planets
around other stars "planets."

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.730
And the astronomer's definition

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.500
wouldn't allow any of those to be planets.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.960
- [Narrator] OK, so basically Stern says

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.930
it depends on context.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.163
But why?

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.490
Back in the early '90s,
Pluto was a planet, period.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.850
No context needed.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.500
So what changed?

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.880
By the late '90s, it was becoming clear

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.350
that Pluto wasn't alone.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.710
Astronomers had discovered other worlds

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.090
in the same region,
called the Kuiper belt.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.820
And some of them looked
awfully similar to Pluto.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:20.260
Then in 2005, astronomers discovered Eris,

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.920
which estimates at the time
suggested was even larger

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.570
than Pluto.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.470
- On January 8 of this year,

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.070
while looking through some old data

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.990
that we had taken with the
Samuel Oschin Telescope

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.578
at Palomar Observatory,

00:02:32.578 --> 00:02:36.140
we found, much to our surprise, an object

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.000
three times further away than Pluto.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.210
This will absolutely rewrite the history

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.860
of astronomy textbooks.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.270
- [Narrator] And while these new worlds

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.410
looked and behaved like Pluto,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.310
they were completely different
from every other planet

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.000
in our solar system.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.910
Something had to be done.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.010
It was clear that astronomers were in need

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.310
of something they never had before:

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.120
a good definition for what
makes a planet a planet.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.650
So in the wake of these new discoveries,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.870
the IAU came up with a checklist.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.970
A planet must orbit the sun,

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.000
have a nearly round shape,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.760
and have cleared its neighborhood,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.900
meaning no other large objects are nearby.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.950
And that last requirement
boots Pluto off Team Planet.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.840
Yes, it orbits the sun.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.800
Yes, it's spherical.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.680
But Pluto isn't always the
dominant gravitational force

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.400
in its neighborhood.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.150
For one thing, Eris shares the region

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.955
and isn't stuck in Pluto's orbit.

00:03:33.955 --> 00:03:36.228
The end result? Pluto is bumped

00:03:36.228 --> 00:03:38.550
from "planet" to "dwarf planet."

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:43.430
Now, Stern argues that a dwarf
planet is a kind of planet.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:44.790
Just like how

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.440
- The bonsai tree is still a tree.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.220
And a Chihuahua is still a dog.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.000
- [Narrator] But other
experts, like Montmerle,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.221
prefer to think of dwarf
planets as their own class.

00:03:54.221 --> 00:03:56.770
So where does that leave us?

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.810
Well, in the grand scheme of things,

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.280
it doesn't really matter
what Pluto's official

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.240
designation is.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.230
- If people don't like it,
they don't use it, period.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.810
- [Narrator] Which is exactly
what planetary scientists do.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.380
- So everyone's using
the planetary scientist's

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:18.047
definition in the written,
refereed scientific literature.

00:04:18.047 --> 00:04:20.540
And using it at the podium

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.603
in giving scientific presentations.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.350
That's the kind of consensus

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.537
that's very powerful in science.

00:04:27.537 --> 00:04:29.670
- [Narrator] So maybe Pluto isn't a planet

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.830
the same way that Earth
and Jupiter are planets.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.490
But that doesn't mean we should ignore it.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.870
Besides, there's more to this dwarf planet

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.760
than meets the eye.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.210
The New Horizons mission
has found evidence

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.220
of ice volcanoes, hidden oceans.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.580
- There are evidence for
icefalls and floes and glaciers.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.930
Just tremendous stuff.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.680
- [Narrator] And that's true,
no matter what you call it.

